From: quickjs-ng object-creation work
Subject: Object creation: extend the inline property path (O1)

What this does
  JS_NewObjectFromShape embeds the property array in the object block only
  for JS_CLASS_OBJECT, JS_CLASS_BYTECODE_FUNCTION and JS_CLASS_FOR_IN_ITERATOR.
  Array and arguments objects, which dominate object creation in the
  ProseMirror Node.fromJSON workload, always took a second js_malloc for their
  property array. Extend the eligible set to JS_CLASS_ARRAY, JS_CLASS_ARGUMENTS
  and JS_CLASS_MAPPED_ARGUMENTS so a small property array is allocated in the
  same block as the object.

  Also add the QJS_ALLOC_STATS measurement counters the object-creation plan
  asks for: objects allocated, inline vs separate property array, property
  array sizes, per-class split, shapes created, property-array growth moves
  and reallocs, cycle-collection runs and objects walked, and for-in
  start/next/has-property counts. Compiled only with -DQJS_ALLOC_STATS; dumped
  at runtime teardown when the QJS_ALLOC_STATS env var is set.

Why we need it
  On the ProseMirror reproducer (tools/benchmark/workloads/95-prosemirror.js)
  JS_CLASS_ARRAY (2-slot length shape) and JS_CLASS_ARGUMENTS (3-slot shape)
  were the entire separate-property-array population: 2,459,523 of 4,333,862
  objects in 200 iterations took the second allocation. With the extension
  that drops to 217 while the remaining property-array work is growth
  (move_out_of_inline 817,632, realloc 445,328). The workload's fromJSON rows
  improve 3-5%.

What it adds
  quickjs.c
      The two class tests in JS_NewObjectFromShape gain JS_CLASS_ARRAY,
      JS_CLASS_ARGUMENTS, JS_CLASS_MAPPED_ARGUMENTS; the QJS_ALLOC_STATS
      counter block and dump.

  Box2D checksum 546.1008391043414 (stack and register VM), test262
  50/81192 with no leaks, differential corpus 398/0/0, ASan on test262-fast
  clean (one pre-existing UBSan allocator-vtable report), peak RSS on box2d
  +0.95%. QJS_ALLOC_STATS is a measurement build only and changes nothing
  when undefined.

